"A B-tree index diagram shows ordered key ranges, internal routing pages, and leaf entries under an explicitly named implementation.\n\nUse when: Explain index lookup, page splits, or range scanning. Review how a database index organizes searchable keys.\n\nAvoid when: The goal is a complete query execution plan.\n\nVisual grammar: Page boxes contain ordered separator keys or leaf entries. Parent-child edges encode routed key ranges. Leaf links are shown only for implementations that maintain them.\n\nBuild: Declare the implementation and whether the drawing is schematic. Choose a small synthetic ordered key set. Build internal separators and leaf ranges consistently. Align tree levels and route optional sibling links below leaf boxes.\n\nCheck: Every child range matches its separator semantics. All leaves have the required balanced depth. Duplicate keys and row references follow the selected implementation. Page borders, key slots, and pointer arrows remain visually distinct.\n\nPitfalls: Mixing textbook B-tree storage with B+ tree leaf-only records without explanation. Claiming an illustrative page layout is an observed physical index.\n\nExample: A product index routes keys below M to one leaf range and keys from M onward to another.\n\nAlternatives: Query execution plan, LSM tree diagram."
